WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.820
Ever since I was a young kid,

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.730
my grandpa tried to instill it in me

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.580
that the coal company is not your friend,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.530
that they're going to exploit you.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.750
They're going to squeeze
every bit of value

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:16.950
out of you that they can,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.380
and they're going to leave you broken.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.610
All you need to do

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.530
is look at this bottle of water

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.840
that I brought with me today.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.870
Congressman Carter, I
talked about orange water.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.853
None of us would drink this.

00:00:33.563 --> 00:00:35.047
They told the people,

00:00:35.047 --> 00:00:36.810
"Don't let your kids play in it

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.540
and don't let your animals drink from it."

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.370
From Pennsylvania and Ohio

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.780
all the way down to Alabama,

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.390
there are roughly 600,000 acres

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.880
of mines left to be reclaimed.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.150
And as they sit, they
create more pollution

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.780
and have more erosion
issues and other problems

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.673
because they're not being maintained.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:58.830
The death toll in Kentucky

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.297
continues to rise following
what the governor called

00:01:01.297 --> 00:01:04.710
"The worst flooding
disaster he has ever seen."

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.500
There's a federal law
that, if you mine coal,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.630
you have to clean up the
land when you're done.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.150
And a lot of companies have found ways

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.443
to avoid doing that.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.730
Bankruptcy seems to provide coal companies

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.850
a way to shed a lot of liability.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.530
Hey, look, there's a stigma associated

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:23.687
with filing for Chapter 11,

00:01:23.687 --> 00:01:25.050
'cause people confuse it

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.580
with you're going out of business.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:27.690
We're not going out of business,

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.130
we're going to restructure.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.800
This is a story about
how big coal companies

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.150
avoid their responsibility

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.683
to clean up their old mines.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.180
My name is Junior Walk. I'm 32 years old.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.223
I work for Coal River Mountain Watch.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.620
Essentially what I do is go out

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.353
and document coal mining operations,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.780
usually with video by drone.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:15.863
Trying to find things

00:02:15.863 --> 00:02:17.790
that the coal companies are doing

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.550
that goes against
environmental regulations,

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.730
like making roads in places
they're not supposed to,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.650
blasting in places
they're not supposed to,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.663
timbering where they're not supposed to,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.543
and get them in trouble on that.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.630
So, welcome, this is the Coal
River Mountain Watch office.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.700
When I was a kid, it
was like a pizza place,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.373
but we've been here since like 2014.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.640
Our main mission is to be a thorn

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.200
in the side of the coal industry.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.083
We are going to keep on them
until they're out of here.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.720
There was a bridge owned by a coal company

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.280
that had collapsed into the river,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.043
and I called it in and
they made them clean it up.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.660
This is the Brushy Fork Impoundment.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.980
It holds back 7.8 billion
gallons of coal slurry.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.873
It's a lake of toxic waste.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.870
The Appalachian Mountains are beautiful.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:16.950
I mean, being up in the mountains,

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.700
it's bright, bright green trees

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.550
and almost vertical mountains

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.436
rising away from the road.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:29.040
At the same time, the
presence of coal there

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.480
is almost overwhelming.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.030
I mean, it's really everywhere.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.630
There are coal conveyors,

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.760
basically big pipes with conveyor belts

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.750
that run over the road.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:40.770
So you're just driving along,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.570
and then there's just a lot of big tubes

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:43.670
running over the road.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.560
There's coal trucks

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.060
that are really, really
loud and really big

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.607
that go by, it seems like
almost like once a minute.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.770
Some of the small towns we stopped in,

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.330
there was coal littering
the side of the road

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.290
because so many coal trucks go by

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:05.910
that coal shakes off the top of them,

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.663
and there was just little
pieces of coal along the road.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.340
A lot of people we talked to

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.160
did feel like, we live
in such a beautiful place

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.230
and it's really been severely damaged

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.120
by the coal companies.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.060
People talked about growing
up with family members

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.370
gathering ginseng or gathering firewood

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.380
or hunting in the woods,

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.420
and that, now, some of those areas

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.310
are just gray moonscapes

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.310
where they can't do any of those things.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.910
This is the Twilight complex,

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:42.990
which is Lexington Coal Company operation.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.510
It's a massive mountaintop removal site

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:50.510
that is a chunk of Raleigh
County and Boone County.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.580
Initially, it was a
Massey Energy operation,

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.830
and then it sold to Alpha.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.780
Then I suppose Alpha has offloaded it

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.110
onto some company known as Lexington

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.670
because they don't want to bother

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.883
with the reclamation
responsibilities up there.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:11.010
This is the site that
Lexington is supposed to be,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.590
you know, reclaiming, and they're not.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.390
There's no equipment out on that site.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.940
No work being done whatsoever.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.560
I mean, it essentially hasn't been touched

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.510
since Alpha had it.

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:29.817
I first got interested in this story

00:05:29.817 --> 00:05:31.563
when I was a bankruptcy reporter.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:38.940
Coal companies went through a big string

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.390
of bankruptcies around 2015, 2016,

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.190
and during that time, they offloaded

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.440
or shed a lot of labor obligations,

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.810
promises that they'd made to pay workers.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.600
And those were mostly picked
up by the federal government.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.430
So I kind of had it in my head that,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.050
you know, coal companies
and their obligations

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.210
was an interesting area.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.180
Bankruptcy seems to provide coal companies

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.720
a way to shed a lot of liability

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.390
and come out the other side

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:10.233
as a profitable company
that still holds mines.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.710
When coal companies go bankrupt,

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.823
any other legal actions
against them are often stopped.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.830
So if we find a water pollution issue

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.340
and it's with a coal company
that is in bankruptcy,

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.530
there's often little we can do

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.710
for citizen enforcement
of water pollution issues

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.910
against a coal company
that's in bankruptcy.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.920
We became focused on a
company called Alpha Met,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.840
Alpha Metallurgical Resources,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.340
because it's a really big company,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.800
it's one of the biggest
coal companies in the U.S.,

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.070
because it's shed a really
large number of permits.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.870
And a mine permit is what a company has

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.760
that allows them to mine coal

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.890
on a specific piece of land.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.300
And attached to the permit

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.763
is also the responsibility
to clean up the land.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:04.950
The story with Alpha

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.050
is they were doing
really well for a while.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.700
They were buying other coal companies,

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:09.533
they were mining a lot of coal,

00:07:09.533 --> 00:07:11.580
they were making a lot of money.

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.660
But around 2015, low natural
gas prices undercut coal,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:17.400
and that helped push companies like Alpha

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.250
and other coal companies into bankruptcy.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.380
Pretty soon after the bankruptcy,

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.200
Alpha passed on its used up mines,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.710
or mines that had less coal
in them, to smaller companies.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.570
And along with those
mines went the obligation

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.620
to clean up the land.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.860
So, over the past decade or so,

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:41.700
really since about 2015
when several companies,

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.890
large companies went bankrupt,

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.510
we've seen a phenomenon

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.333
of companies shedding their liabilities.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.170
Essentially, they will
sell their good mines

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.060
that still have coal to mine

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.670
to companies that want those mines,

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.310
and then they will sell their other mines

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.620
that often only need reclamation

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:06.660
and may have a lot of liability

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.170
to other coal companies
who agree to take them on,

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.540
usually because the original companies

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.490
are paying them to do so.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.970
And we've seen several
rounds of that happen.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.130
It's kind of like a game of hot potato

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:23.490
where we're just waiting
to see what coal company

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:26.130
is going to be left with the mine

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.080
when they eventually run out of money

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.390
and can no longer find
another coal company

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:31.770
to take them on.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.440
Alpha Natural Resources
announced the transfer

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.720
of some mostly idled mining properties

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.390
in West Virginia, Kentucky, and Tennessee

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.150
to a company called
Lexington Coal Company.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:44.010
Almost all the big coal
companies in the U.S.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:45.780
have used a similar playbook.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.220
The big mining companies, like Alpha,

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.980
transferred a lot of their
permits to smaller companies,

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.550
companies like Lexington,
Blackjewel, Pristine.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:55.050
And then those smaller companies

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.510
have often racked up a really large number

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.730
of environmental violations.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.240
So when Alpha and Lexington
announced the big transfer,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.310
they said that they could
accelerate reclamation

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:06.510
on a five-year timeline.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.113
Five years have passed and
not a lot has been done.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.170
Out of about 230 permits,

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.733
only 40 have been cleaned up.

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:39.223
All righty, here we go.

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.020
So when I was a senior in high school,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.600
I went to work for Massey Coal Company,

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.580
'cause I realized that
you kind of need money

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.170
to go to college in this country,

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.950
and I didn't have money
and neither did my family.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.870
So I went to work with my dad

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.660
at the Elk Run coal processing plant.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:05.160
I was just doing general maintenance.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.630
Some days it wasn't so bad,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.270
they just have me like cutting grass.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:12.030
But some days, I'd be
waist deep in coal slurry

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.580
with a high-powered pressure hose.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.800
I felt like I was taking
my life into my own hands

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.200
every time I'd come to work.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:22.950
So I quit that job, and
by the time I was 19,

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.840
a friend of the family
offered me a position

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.590
as a security guard on a
mountaintop removal site.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:32.340
But within the first week
of me working up there,

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:34.950
watching that machinery
tear down that mountain

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.080
for 12 hours a day made me feel

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.030
like that money I was putting in my wallet

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:42.120
was blood money, because it was.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.360
I mean, the things I went
through when I was a kid

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.880
of coal trucks rumbling by your house

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.760
all hours of the night, coal trains,

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.460
you know, dust, bad water, all that,

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:54.870
I knew that the people who were living

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.300
down below that mine site
that I was working on

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.050
were going through the same things.

00:11:06.307 --> 00:11:08.250
This is the underground coal mine

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:11.103
near my parents' house in Eunice.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.560
You can just see coal pouring

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.140
right off the belt line
into the coal pile.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:19.893
You can see an excavator
loading up a coal truck.

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.800
When I was a real young kid,

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:30.990
we had well water at this place,

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:33.360
and Massey Energy, at the time,

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.360
started getting rid of their
coal slurry by pumping it

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.090
into an old abandoned
underground coal mine

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:40.590
on this same hillside

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:42.290
that this new coal mine is now in.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.050
That ended up making its
way through the groundwater,

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.480
through the aquifer, and
getting into our well.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.860
And our well water at that
house was red for years

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.233
and smelled absolutely disgusting.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:58.530
Yeah, this crap over here

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.930
pretty much ruined my childhood home,

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:01.833
so that's great.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:14.190
In these underground deposits,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.050
West Virginia has coal enough

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.753
to last the nation for generations.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.140
Coal really powered the U.S.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:23.400
Coal was used to forge weapons

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.320
during the Revolutionary War.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.750
But for most of the first 200 years

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:28.980
coal was mined in the U.S.,

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.140
there were no real regulations or laws

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.570
that would make companies
clean up the land

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.880
after they were finished mining.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:39.210
That changed in 1977 with
the Surface Mining Control

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:41.640
and Reclamation Act, SMCRA.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:44.250
I recommend a three-phase program

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:45.990
at the federal level.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:49.080
One is a law that says industry

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.440
cannot strip mine land
unless it can restore it

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.553
to its original utility
and natural purpose.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.250
So the federal surface mining law

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.650
spells out certain requirements

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:10.020
about returning the land

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.513
to its approximate original contour,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:15.780
so that means putting some

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:17.730
of that excess dirt and rock back,

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.100
kind of building the mountain back up.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.530
They would have to re-vegetate the site

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.530
or add whatever
infrastructure might be needed

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:26.670
for post-mining land use.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.700
So in a lot of cases, it
would be replanting forests,

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.673
but it could also be
building infrastructure.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:37.350
They also have to address water problems.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.800
During mining, they
control their water runoff

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:41.850
with sediment ponds

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:43.710
so that they're not letting polluted water

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:45.180
leave the mines.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:48.240
But during reclamation, they are required

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.490
to get that mine back to the state

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.983
where it's no longer
producing polluted water.

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.250
Most companies would claim

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.170
that they're doing good reclamation

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:01.560
and they're doing it
as quickly as they can.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.780
But from what we've seen on the ground,

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.420
we see mines that sit unreclaimed

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.340
for close to a decade.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.500
So those mines aren't being reclaimed

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.750
and they are not producing coal.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.670
They're just sitting idled.

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:17.910
And as they sit, they
create more pollution

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:21.570
and have more erosion
issues and other problems

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.463
because they're not being maintained.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.810
Eastern Kentucky is the epicenter tonight

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:33.813
of the nation's latest
extreme weather disaster.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.070
The death toll in Kentucky

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.017
continues to rise following
what the governor called,

00:14:41.017 --> 00:14:44.370
quote, "the worst flooding
disaster he has ever seen."

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:48.033
There are hundreds of
unaccounted-for people, minimum.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.150
The flooding that we've seen

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:53.580
in Central Appalachia in recent years

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.730
as weather events get more extreme,

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:59.550
that flooding has been exacerbated

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:02.130
by the presence of these
unreclaimed surface mines,

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:06.270
because the mines that
haven't been re-vegetated,

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:11.270
they can't hold water
like a natural forest can.

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:14.460
One of the greatest sources of frustration

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.770
is that, while there are laws on the books

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:20.010
that actually require the coal companies

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.440
to do remediation and to clean up

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.500
some of the damage that this process does,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.000
those laws are rarely enforced.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.360
And there millions and
millions of dollars in fines

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.120
that have been levied
against these coal companies

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.260
that they haven't paid.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:35.970
So the sad truth is

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:38.250
that the cost of this damage is borne

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:40.279
by the people who live in these areas.

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.320
It was a really difficult story to report.

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:52.500
Some of the challenges
were people in the region.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:54.480
People in West Virginia and Kentucky

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:57.150
were often pretty reluctant to speak

00:15:57.150 --> 00:15:58.620
about what they'd been through

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:00.420
or how their homes had been damaged.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:04.410
Coal provides many of the jobs,

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.510
or at least it has
historically in the region.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:08.940
So people felt like they'd be speaking out

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:11.190
against their uncle or cousin or dad

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:12.180
who worked at a coal mine,

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.320
or they'd be getting dirty looks

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.150
at a grocery store.

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:16.530
But I think one moment

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.274
when it felt like the story
really clicked into place

00:16:19.274 --> 00:16:21.624
is when I talked to a
man named Miles Hatfield.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.250
He lives in Eastern Kentucky.

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:31.470
He lives sort of downstream or downhill

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.500
from an old Alpha mine called Love Branch.

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:36.546
And the mine was transferred to Lexington

00:16:36.546 --> 00:16:39.993
as part of the big, as part
of the big asset transfer.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.880
And he complained, even
before the transfer,

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:46.923
that there was flooding onto his land.

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.393
It had made his yard
into sort of a red marsh.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:55.380
But the transfer to
Lexington went through,

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:57.810
and just as Alpha had
not cleaned up the land

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.030
or stopped the flooding of his home,

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.010
Lexington didn't stop it either

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.873
and he ended up having to move.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:06.780
To me, that was a perfect example

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:10.470
of a mine that had been owned by Alpha

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.150
and transferred to Lexington

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.430
and not cleaned up by anyone

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:15.600
and then was hurting someone

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:17.000
that lived right next to it.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.310
At that time, I think it was named Alpha,

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.053
but they switch back and forth.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.900
To my understanding, they jump in and out

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:32.214
of bankruptcy a lot to avoid some things.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.470
The water started coming through

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:40.143
probably within a year
after they quit mining.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.153
My dining room kitchen fell in.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.213
There wasn't no warning when it fell.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.770
In a bat of an eye, I was
in four foot of water,

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:58.623
which I didn't know was
there until I went in it.

00:18:00.497 --> 00:18:01.830
It took me about 20 minutes

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:04.083
to get out of the hole that I was in,

00:18:05.146 --> 00:18:07.230
and I went upstairs and told my wife.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:08.160
She couldn't believe it.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.250
She come down and where
we did have a kitchen

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.803
and dining room, we
didn't have it anymore.

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:19.020
When that floor fell, that
took all the water lines out.

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:20.433
So we didn't have water.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:22.890
We couldn't really live down here,

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.500
so we got lucky and got to rent a house

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.150
about a mile down the road there.

00:18:28.611 --> 00:18:30.960
But there's nothing to do up here.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.333
I keep the grass cut, and that's about it.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:40.500
I would appreciate if they would step in

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.113
and take care of what damage they did.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.950
They can't deny it,
because the scientists,

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:48.960
they were here three different times,

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:52.710
but they did say this
was from that coal mine.

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:56.343
They said it was their water
that's done this damage.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.033
Not all coal companies are that bad,

00:19:01.244 --> 00:19:04.603
it's just some of them
get the coal and get out.

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:19.830
The problem with SMCRA at this point

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:23.970
is that the requirements
for reclamation are unclear

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:26.040
and there isn't enough money available

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:28.080
to do the reclamation.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:32.040
It's $11.3 billion that
taxpayers are paying

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.890
for coal companies that
went out of business

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:37.020
and did not leave money around

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.260
for the cleanup of what they left behind.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:42.480
It's really easy for coal companies

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.310
to convince state regulators

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:46.470
to give them a lot of leeway

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:49.323
for how long it takes
them to do reclamation.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.610
The DEP, Department of
Environmental Protection

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:54.660
here in West Virginia,

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:57.390
is a state agency, and as such,

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:59.490
they're ran by the state government.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:02.490
Well, who in this state
runs the state government?

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.110
It's the coal industry.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.090
And so, in that way, the coal industry

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:08.523
is much like a a self-regulating entity.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:15.123
So we're going up Kayford
Mountain Mountain right now.

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:19.650
Really, Kayford is a perfect example

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:23.103
to show folks of how
reclamation is a farce,

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.870
because there's no way you can build back

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.150
one of the most biologically
diverse ecosystems

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.280
on the entire planet with bulldozers

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.170
and the same hydro seed you see

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:35.943
on the sides of interstates.

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.530
Hydro seed, it's a grass
seed mixed with fertilizer,

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:43.590
and like chopped up
newspaper, essentially.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:45.990
But that stuff will grow
on literally anything.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:47.400
You could spray that on my desk

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:48.540
and it it'd be ready to cut

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:50.013
with a lawnmower next week.

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:54.090
This is all the reclamation
or remediation work

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:56.100
that they're even liable for.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:59.103
So it looks pretty awful, if you ask me.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.710
The way the laws are structured,

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:08.370
if you dig ginseng before
the 1st of September,

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:09.780
that's federal time.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.730
That's big, big trouble

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.400
because it's a rare
plant, it's endangered.

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:17.490
But these coal companies, all year round,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.290
can blow off the tops of these mountains,

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:22.020
some of the most lush, dense forests

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:23.640
I've ever seen in my life, you know,

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:26.190
some of the most prime ginseng habitat

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:29.940
in the entire planet
just totally turned into

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.013
what amounts to a parking lot.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.570
The mountain may not
be able to be put back

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:41.343
exactly like it once was,

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:46.020
but people can do good reclamation.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.200
And good reclamation is
unquestionably better

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.733
than no reclamation.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:57.570
So we would like to see
good reclamation done

00:21:57.570 --> 00:21:58.983
at all of these mines.

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:06.170
A lot of these mines can
become productive forest again.

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:10.620
But it takes effort and
time and money to do that,

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.900
and a lot of the coal companies

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:15.663
want to avoid putting that much effort in.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:19.680
I think that it is very likely

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:23.040
there will need to be federal funding

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:24.990
to cover at least some

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:27.513
of the reclamation that needs to be done.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:33.510
Most people feel like
coal is on a decline.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.820
We're shutting down
coal plants in the U.S.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:38.160
The idea is that we'll be
using less and less coal

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:40.143
since it's such a dirty fossil fuel.

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:43.680
That said, we're in an
energy crisis right now

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:45.580
and there's a lot of coal being mined.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.920
A lot of these companies
are seeing record profits.

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:52.920
Alpha, for example, is doing great.

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:57.183
Its stock is up over 700%
from when it left bankruptcy.

